<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Rhizome connects to local model servers you configure for AI chat.</string>
  <!--
    macOS gates Documents, Desktop and Downloads behind TCC, and only prompts
    for one if the matching usage string is declared here. Without these keys
    there is no prompt and no way to grant access from inside the app.

    These are load-bearing, not boilerplate: Rhizome passes the vault path to
    Prime as the session working directory, so a vault in a protected folder
    makes the Prime session worker die at launch on process.cwd() (EPERM,
    uv_cwd). The supervisor then waits 30s for a worker that never connects and
    chat fails with a generic timeout that reads like a model problem. C53.

    A vault in ~/Documents is the common case, so Documents is the one that
    matters most; Desktop and Downloads are declared for the same reason.
  -->
  <key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Rhizome needs access to your Documents folder to open a vault stored there.</string>
  <key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Rhizome needs access to your Desktop folder to open a vault stored there.</string>
  <key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Rhizome needs access to your Downloads folder to open a vault stored there.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
